    "issued_at": "2026-08-21T08:57:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "8723d5b4-2021-4c71-9c9d-179f2eba5843",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 210857\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n157 AM MST Fri Aug 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Highs will return to around 110 degrees today across the lower\n  deserts and continue into early next week before heating up to\n  around 114 degrees during the latter half of next week. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings have been extended through next Friday\n  evening due to continued areas of major HeatRisk through early\n  next week and increasing to widespread Extreme HeatRisk during\n  the latter half of next week.\n\n- Isolated thunderstorms are expected to remain confined to the \n  high terrain through at least Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nThe subtropical high is currently centered over the Four Corners\nregion early this morning and models are in good agreement that it\nwill slowly progress eastward through tomorrow and eventually\nsettle over eastern CO/NM. This will cause winds over our region\nto become more southerly, which will usher in much drier air.\nPWATs early this morning currently range from 1.0-1.5\" over our\narea and models show PWATs decreasing to 0.9-1.3\". Models also\nshow mixing ratios as low as 7-8 g/kg. This will result in dry\nconditions across the lower deserts and only slight chances for\nsome isolated showers and storms over the higher terrain areas.\nModels do show slight chances of a weak outflow boundary trying to\nmove into the lower deserts. However, any outflows are expected to\nwash out as they move off the terrain, similar to last night. \n\nDespite the high shifting eastward over the next couple of days,\nmodels show 500mb heights of 593-596dm over our area. Skies will\nreturn to mostly clear by this afternoon, which will allow\ntemperatures to heat back up today and are forecasted to be around\n110-114 (98-108) degrees across the lower deserts (higher \nterrain) this afternoon. Similar temperatures are forecasted for\nSaturday and may be a degree or two warmer than today in some\nlocations. These hot temperatures will result in widespread Major\nHeatRisk across the lower deserts and in some areas of the higher\nterrain (specifically the San Carlos area). As a result, the\nExtreme Heat Warning for the lower deserts and San Carlos area\nremains in effect. Make sure to practice smart heat safety by\ndrinking plenty of water and electrolytes and limiting your time\noutdoors, especially during the afternoons.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nModel uncertainty remains quite high for the first half of next \nweek as the position of the high is likely to set up somewhere \nover eastern New Mexico and western Texas by Monday. This \nposition can cause higher uncertainty in moisture levels for our \narea with the latest guidance trying to advect some moisture into \nour area by Sunday or Monday, but NBM forecast PoPs are at most \n10-15% for the lower deserts. At this point, we could continue to \nsee mostly slight chances favoring the high terrain into early \nnext week, but if moisture does eventually improve enough storm \nchances are likely to be higher than what the NBM is depicting. \nAnother wildcard will be the potential influence of an upper level\ntrough that may attempt to move into western portions of our \nregion Sunday into Monday. If this occurs, it may be enough to \nimprove convective potential across southeast California and \nwestern Arizona for Sunday and/or Monday. By Tuesday, drier air \nwill filter back into the region as the subtropical high begins to\nretrograde back over our region. This will end limit low end rain\nchances to the higher terrain areas with the lower deserts\nremaining dry. By the latter half of next week, the NBM shows PoPs\nbeing confined to the Mogollon Rim with no chances for our CWA. \n\nTemperatures are expected to continue to be more influenced by the\nstrong subtropical high as H5 heights hover between 593-596dm\nacross the Desert Southwest through early next week. Daytime highs\nare not expected to change much from Sunday to Monday, barring \nany significant change with the convective chances. As mentioned\nabove, by Tuesday the subtropical high will start to retrograde \nback to the west and center will eventually settle over AZ/NM \nduring the latter half of the workweek. The high will also \nstrengthen to around 595-597dm during this time. The combination \nof the center settling over our region and strengthening will \nresult in temperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) will \nbe in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the lower deserts \nand in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range across the higher terrain \nareas. The increase in temperatures will also result in an \nincrease in HeatRisk, with widespread Extreme HeatRisk forecasted \nfor the majority of the CWA for the latter half of the workweek. \nDue to these hot temperatures and widespread Extreme HeatRisk \nexpected next week, the Extreme Heat Warning has been extended \nthrough next Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0510Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected throughout the TAF\nperiod under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks. The overall wind\npattern will follow the typical diurnal tendencies with overall\nspeeds remaining aob 10 kts. Wind directions do become a bit more\nuncertain Friday evening as there are some indications from the\nhi-res guidance that storm activity across southeast AZ may send \na decaying outflow boundary into the area, with a potential wind \nshift out of the south-southwest to south. However, at this time, \nno significant gusts are anticipated if the outflow comes to \nfruition. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected throughout the TAF\nperiod under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks. Winds will generally\nfluctuate out of the southeast to southwest direction with speeds\nunder 12 kts. Periods of light variability will be common during \nthe early morning hours. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air will filter into the region today and Saturday with \neven lower chances for rain while temperatures heat back up to \n5 to 8 degrees above normal. Only slight chances for isolated\nstorms exists over the higher terrain through Saturday. MinRHs of\n15-25% today decrease to 12-20% tomorrow. MinRh then increases\nagain to 15-25% Sunday and Monday before decreasing back to 10-20%\nbeginning Tuesday. Overnight recovery will generally be in the\n30-60% range, with the best night being Sunday night. Winds \nshould overall follow the typical diurnal tendencies with some \nmodest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>544-546-\n     548>551-553>555-559-560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich/Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Lojero \nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich\n",
